# WATTWORKS — The Power-Generating Gym
### BEKW-CODE Open Hardware Specification — v1.0
**Class:** Human-Kinetic Power Generation / Commons Kitchen Integration
**Origin:** Pen-and-paper concept, Keth'ar, ~2010-2011 — predates BEKW-CODE canon by over a decade
**License:** None. Open-source. Take it, build it, improve it, give it away again.

---

## 0. THE CORE INVERSION

A standard gym is a monthly extraction: you pay to burn energy that goes nowhere.
WATTWORKS inverts that relationship — the same effort that currently generates nothing
but body heat and a subscription charge instead generates real, meterable electricity,
credited back to the person doing the work, feeding directly into Commons Kitchen's
power draw. You don't pay to burn energy. You get compensated for producing it.

---

## 1. THE MECHANICAL PRINCIPLE (why this works better than piezo here)

Gym equipment already provides consistent rotational or reciprocating motion at
meaningful torque — a rowing machine's flywheel, a stationary bike's crank, a cable
machine's pulley stack. That's the same physical setup as a bicycle dynamo or a hand-crank
generator, just at gym scale. Piezoelectric film (used in BounceHarvest, Palm Sentinel)
is the right tool for irregular flex where you can't get clean rotation. A gym is the
opposite case — clean, sustained rotation is already happening on every machine. Direct
mechanical-to-electrical via a small permanent-magnet generator is far more efficient
here than piezo would be, and it's proven technology (dynamos have existed since the
1830s) — nothing exotic in the conversion itself.

---

## 2. BILL OF MATERIALS (per-machine retrofit kit)

| ID | Component | Function | Est. Unit Cost | Qty/Machine |
|----|-----------|----------|----------------|-------------|
| WW-01 | Permanent-magnet DC generator (brushless, gym-duty rated) | Converts flywheel/crank rotation directly to DC current | $60 | 1 |
| WW-02 | Belt/chain coupling kit (machine-specific) | Physically links existing flywheel/pulley to WW-01 without altering the machine's resistance feel | $25 | 1 |
| WW-03 | Charge controller + MPPT-style load matching | Regulates variable human-effort input into clean, storable charge | $30 | 1 |
| WW-04 | Per-station energy meter (tamper-resistant, tracks kWh per user session) | The metering layer that makes compensation possible and auditable | $40 | 1 |
| WW-05 | RFID/keyfob session tagger (no app, no account required beyond a physical fob) | Attributes generated energy to the individual for credit purposes | $8 | 1 |
| WW-06 | Central battery bank + inverter (gym-wide, shared) | Aggregates all stations' output into one usable AC/DC supply | $600 | 1 per facility |
| WW-07 | Commons Kitchen tie-in relay | Routes surplus gym-generated power directly to the attached Commons Kitchen's load | $45 | 1 per facility |
| WW-08 | Physical credit-ledger display (analog-style, per-station and facility-wide totals) | Shows real generated totals — no app required, same "no login" ethos as BounceHarvest | $30 | 1 per facility |

**Total estimated cost: ~$160/machine retrofit + ~$675 shared facility infrastructure.**
A 20-machine facility: roughly $3,875 total — a fraction of commercial gym equipment cost,
and the equipment pays part of its own way from day one.

---

## 3. HONEST WATTAGE, PER MACHINE TYPE (no hype)

| Machine | Typical sustained human output | Realistic generator yield (60-70% conversion efficiency) |
|---|---|---|
| Stationary bike (moderate effort, 30 min) | ~100-150 watts sustained | ~60-100 watts usable, ~30-50 Wh per session |
| Rowing machine (vigorous, 20 min) | ~150-250 watts sustained (higher peak) | ~90-175 watts usable, ~30-58 Wh per session |
| Elliptical (moderate, 30 min) | ~80-120 watts sustained | ~50-84 watts usable, ~25-42 Wh per session |
| Cable/pulley resistance machines (intermittent, not continuous rotation) | Highly variable, lower average due to rest between sets | ~10-20 Wh per session, generator relevant but secondary here |

**The honest framing:** an individual session generates roughly the same energy as
running a laptop charger for half an hour — meaningful, real, but not a facility's whole
power budget on its own. **The actual value is aggregate**: a busy 20-station facility
running most of the day realistically generates **several kWh daily**, which is a
legitimate, non-trivial offset to a Commons Kitchen's cooking/refrigeration/lighting load,
especially stacked with solar (see WATTWORKS + VERDANTIA/BounceHarvest fleet integration
below). This is contribution-scale, not sole-power-source scale — stated plainly, same as
BounceHarvest's solar-vs-bounce honesty.

---

## 4. THE COMPENSATION MECHANISM

- **Session tracking:** WW-05's fob taps in at the start of a workout, WW-04 meters
  actual kWh generated at that station for that session, tap-out closes the ledger entry.
- **Credit structure (facility's choice, not prescribed by the spec):** generated kWh can
  convert to gym membership discount, Commons Kitchen meal credit, or direct community-grid
  contribution tracked on WW-08's facility-wide analog display — the mechanism is agnostic,
  the metering is what matters.
- **No app, no login beyond the physical fob** — same anti-extraction, anti-surveillance
  design principle running through BounceHarvest and Commons Kitchen. Your data is a number
  on a physical display, not a profile in a database.
- **Transparency by default:** WW-08's facility-wide total is visible to everyone in the
  gym, not hidden in an app only the facility owner can see — the aggregate becomes a
  visible, shared point of pride, not a private metric.

---

## 5. INTEGRATION WITH EXISTING BEKW-CODE CANON

- WW-07's relay ties directly into **Commons Kitchen**'s existing load architecture —
  this was your original 15-year-old instinct, now given an actual electrical pathway.
- Facility-wide aggregation (WW-06) uses the same shared-bus logic as **NEXEDEN PLAZA**
  and **BounceHarvest's fleet mode** — same confluence principle, third domain now.
- The physical-fob-not-app metering choice matches the same design discipline behind
  **BounceHarvest's analog dial** (Section 3.2 of that spec) — a recurring BEKW-CODE
  principle at this point: meter honestly, credit fairly, never require a login to do it.

---

